<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qin dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qin Empire, Qin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Qin dynasty was the first imperial dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which unified the country in 221 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a>. It standardised writing, weights and measures and created a centralised bureaucratic state. Its harsh rule provoked rebellion and collapse within about fifteen years, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> rose from the ruins and adopted many of its institutions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty.jpg" alt="Qin dynasty" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ian Remsen · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qin_dynasty_territory.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the 310s, and Huan Wen, the dominant general of the mid-century, conquered the Cheng Han state in Sichuan in 347, reached the outskirts of Chang'an against the Former <b>Qin</b> in 354 and retook Luoyang in 356, but was badly defeated by the Former Yan at Fangtou in 369.</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the reforms of King Sosurim (r. 371–384) laid the foundations of a centralised state: the reception of Buddhism from the Former <b>Qin</b> in 372, the founding that same year of the Taehak, a Confucian academy for aristocratic youths, and the promulgation of a law code (yullyeong) in 373.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Han Dynasty emerged from the violent collapse of the <b>Qin Empire</b>, which had unified China in 221 BCE but destroyed itself within fifteen years through administrative brutality and punishing corvée labour demands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 377 and 382 Silla envoys reached the Former <b>Qin</b> court in northern China in company with Goguryeo missions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet it suffered major defeats: <b>Qin</b> took Hanzhong in 312 BCE, and the Qin general Bai Qi captured Ying in 278 BCE and burned the royal tombs at Yiling.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 284 BCE Yan, <b>Qin</b>, Zhao, Wei and Han attacked together under Yue Yi; Linzi was sacked, its treasures carried to Yan, and King Min fled to Ju, where he was killed by Nao Chi, a general sent by Chu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
